.TH "SKARN-CMDS" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-cmds \- Show the shell commands run across sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBcmds\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Show the shell commands run across sessions.
.PP
Shell commands run across sessions. Commands, their output and the labels are
printed with detected secrets masked, while \-\-cmd and \-\-failed match the
raw text. For Kiro CLI, cmds lists agent shell calls with a command string:
execute_bash and Windows execute_cmd in the Classic database, shell in V2, and
execute_bash and Windows execute_pwsh in V3. Skarn never reads the Classic
history table of user shell activity; tools still lists shell calls whose
arguments contain no command string.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-cli\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to one assistant's sessions. One of: claude, gemini, codex, cursor,
copilot, kimi, grok, grokbot, antigravity, opencode, cline, copilot\-code,
kiro.
.TP
\fB\-\-project\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to matching projects: a comma\-separated list of names or paths,
matched case\-insensitively against a session's project display name, its
canonical root path, or a parent directory of that root. A session read from a
Compliance API export carries no project, so any \-\-project value excludes
it.
.TP
\fB\-\-worktree\fR \fIpath\fR
Restrict to sessions whose working directory is under this path.
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIdate\fR
Lower bound of the time window, applied to a session file's last\-modified
time rather than to when the session started, so a session begun before the
bound but written to inside it is included and a resumed session stays visible
under the day you worked on it. Takes priority over \-\-hours. Naming one
session by id changes this: `skarn search \-\-session <id>`, `skarn stats
<id>` and the other per\-session views ignore the window altogether, and
`skarn messages <id>` is the single command that applies it there, reading
BOTH bounds against the session's own start time rather than its file and
refusing an out\-of\-window session with exit 1 instead of filtering it out. A
bare YYYY\-MM\-DD means that day's midnight, shifted by \-\-tz\-offset; a full
ISO timestamp is used as given.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIdate\fR
Upper bound of the time window, applied to when a session STARTED rather than
to when its file was last written, so a session that began after the bound is
excluded even if it was already running before it. The two bounds therefore
read different timestamps. Passing it without \-\-since also drops the
\-\-hours lower bound, so the window becomes all history up to the bound. A
bare YYYY\-MM\-DD covers that whole day through 23:59:59, shifted by
\-\-tz\-offset; a full ISO timestamp is used as given. A session whose start
time cannot be parsed is kept. A view that names one session by id ignores
this bound, except `skarn messages <id>`, which refuses a session past it with
exit 1 rather than dropping it from a list.
.TP
\fB\-\-tz\-offset\fR \fIminutes\fR
Timezone offset in minutes for date grouping and display. The default is 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-hours\fR \fIn\fR
Time window in hours; 0 means no limit. The default is 168.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
Output format. One of: text, json. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-\-session\fR \fIid\fR
Restrict to a single session by id (prefix\-matched).
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbose output.
.TP
\fB\-\-failed\fR
Only show entries that failed (errored or exited non\-zero).
.TP
\fB\-\-unique\fR
Collapse duplicates, keeping the latest occurrence.
.TP
\fB\-\-cmd\fR \fItext\fR
Case\-insensitive substring filter on the command text.
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIname\fR
Restrict to matching users: a comma\-separated list of user ids or email
addresses, where an id matches exactly and an email address matches
case\-insensitively. Only a session read from a Compliance API export carries
either, so on a machine without an export, or without the Enterprise license
that reading one needs, every \-\-user value matches nothing.
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\-home\fR \fIdir\fR
Read a copied home's default session stores (Pro). For configuration
discovery, Skarn reads the copied home's own assistant configuration. Skarn
looks where this machine's operating system keeps each store, so a copy taken
on another operating system can show fewer session or configuration rows. The
Compliance API export root resolves under dir too, and reading it still needs
Enterprise. Session discovery ignores store overrides, XDG_DATA_HOME,
XDG_CONFIG_HOME and SKARN_COMPLIANCE_EXPORT_DIR. The license, EULA record,
~/.skarn.json, caches and outputs stay with the examiner. Skarn ignores the
copied .skarn.json. Skarn resolves ~/ in sessions_path under dir and reports
skipped absolute paths. All history is in scope unless you specify \-\-hours,
\-\-since or \-\-until. Only an explicit \-\-baseline applies. Assess reads
the copied home's saved permission rules for saved_approvals when in scope.
Vet reads only home\-layer configuration and refuses \-\-all\-projects and
\-\-project\-only. Inventory reads sessions and home\-layer configuration. Its
\-\-project filter applies only to sessions. Check writes the same
skarn.inventory line with \-\-format ndjson and refuses \-\-drop,
\-\-audit\-verify and \-\-baseline\-merge. Restore omits cd and names the
evidence machine's unchecked directory on stderr. The session scan skips
transcript path checks, reads derived metadata only under dir and follows no
link below the evidence home: each refused link counts once as an unreadable
store, and one stderr line per reason gives the count. Configuration discovery
in vet and inventory follows no symbolic link below dir either. Both accept a
plain UNC share as the evidence home and exit 6 for a refused network or
device spelling or a control character. Skarn refuses outputs under dir and
homes that contain the examiner's home or overlap Skarn's config or cache
directories (exit 1). Missing, unreadable or non\-directory paths, paths
containing *, and paths that pass through a link to a network path, a link
Skarn cannot resolve or a component Skarn cannot check for links cause exit 6;
the stderr line names the link. Licensing failures exit 7 (no usable license)
or 5 (insufficient tier) before evidence access. Input files can reside
anywhere. JSON, SARIF, NDJSON and the audit record have no dedicated
evidence\-home field. Finding paths show the directory. The evidence pack
names it on one line. For check, \-\-fail\-on\-scan\-error fails incomplete
scans. Requires a Pro license.
